  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nCurrently, ridging remains centered over the Southern Plains and the \nLower Mississippi Valley with a trough located over the Pacific \nNorthwest slowly marching eastward. At the surface, a low pressure \nis developing ahead of this trough across the Central High Plains \ninto Nebraska. Anomalous heights over the region are leading to near \nrecord warmth over the forecast area as highs reach into the upper \n90s to around 100 degrees in central Kansas. As the pressure \ngradient tightens across the state in response to the deepening low \nto our northwest, strong southwesterly winds are being observed \nacross the region. Sustained winds between 15-25 mph with gusts up \nto 35 mph are expected through the early evening hours with the \nhighest winds across central Kansas. Breezy conditions will continue \ninto the overnight hours with the approaching low. \n\nFor Saturday, as the frontal boundary moves into the hot and humid \nenvironment ahead of it, storms are expected to fire across central \nKansas during the afternoon and move southeastward into the evening \nhours. Afternoon highs on Saturday will reach into the middle to \nupper 90s across the area before the front moves through Modest \ninstability (MLCAPE around 2000 J/kg) and decent shear of around 30 \nkts will lead to a few severe storms developing. Strong winds and \nisolated large hail will be the primary threats, along with locally \nheavy rainfall. Storms will gradually diminish in intensity on \nSaturday evening/night, though the heavy rain threat will continue \nacross portions of Kansas. By early Sunday morning, the front will \nbe draped across far eastern/southeastern Kansas. Some showers and \nstorms will linger throughout the day on Sunday, but any strong to \nsevere storm potential will be confined to the far southeast corner \nof the state. Highs on Sunday will range widely as the cooler \ntemperatures will lag behind the front some. Highs in the 70s are \nexpected for central Kansas with highs in the lower 90s will be seen \nfor far southern and southeast Kansas.\n\nFor next week, cooler temperatures will overspread much of the \ncenter part of the country and highs will actually top out slightly \nbelow normal for the beginning of the week. Highs in the 70s and \nlower 80s are expected with lows in the 50s and low 60s. Mostly dry \nconditions are expected in the wake of the frontal boundary as high \npressure sets up over the region. The next rain chances look to \narrive towards the middle to end of next week as a weak wave \ntraverses the Plains.",
